\code{k * T / n_frames}, starting from the previous call's \code{final_grid} -- the
Gillespie process is Markovian, so this produces a trajectory
statistically identical to one continuous run.

A single Gillespie step cannot be split: \code{\link[=simulate_spatial_from_grid]{simulate_spatial_from_grid()}}'s
engine always applies whatever reaction it draws next in full, even when
that reaction's waiting time overshoots the requested horizon (there is
no way to ask it "how much time until the next event, without applying
it" -- that would need a change to the underlying C++, which this
function deliberately avoids). So a single call can overshoot its
requested checkpoint by a lot, especially early on or with slow rate
constants (no reaction is possible faster than the model's own smallest
active rate allows) -- e.g. requesting \code{T = 10} sliced into 30 frames
asks for one reaction roughly every \code{0.33} time units, but if the
landscape's next actual reaction is \code{12} time units away (very plausible
with, say, \code{default_rates()}'s regrowth/replacement rates, which the
paper itself only shows producing visible change over \code{T} on the order
of \code{100}-\code{300}), that one call alone jumps past \code{12/0.33 ~ 36} requested
checkpoints at once. This function handles that correctly: whenever a
call's actual elapsed time (\code{time_sim}) reaches or passes one or more
pending checkpoints, ALL of them are filled with that call's resulting
grid (correctly showing no change happened yet), and the next call
starts from the next still-unfilled checkpoint -- so the total number of
engine calls actually made is bounded by the number of \emph{real} reactions
needed to cover \verb{[0, T]}, which can be far fewer than \code{n_frames} for slow
dynamics (efficient) or up to \code{n_frames} for fast ones (e.g. an active
fire), never more. The practical takeaway: if you see very few distinct
frames (or a \code{verbose} log with very few calls before reaching \code{T}),
that's the model genuinely not doing much in \verb{[0, T]} at these rates, not
a bug -- pick a larger \code{T} (see \code{\link[=default_rates]{default_rates()}}'s documentation and
the paper's own figures for typical horizons) rather than a smaller
\code{n_frames}.

Each engine call gets its own derived seed (\verb{seed + <call index>}, when
\code{seed} is supplied) rather than reusing one seed repeatedly, which would
otherwise restart the underlying C RNG from the same state on every
call.

If the native population goes extinct (or the process reaches an
absorbing state, \code{sumA == 0}) before \code{T}, matching \code{\link[=simulate_spatial]{simulate_spatial()}}'s
own \code{check_extinction} early stop, all remaining checkpoints are filled
with that final grid, so the rendered video still runs the full
requested length, ending on a frozen final state, instead of silently
being shorter than asked.
